.playwright-mcp/

# outputs/ is an allowlist, not a blocklist. The CSV tables here are the numbers the
# README and docs/ quote, so they are tracked: a result nobody can see is not a result,
# and a change that moves a flux by half a percent should show up in a diff rather than
# only on the author's disk. Everything else generated into this directory -- the fitted
# .npz stress models, PNG figures, anything new -- stays out, because it is binary, it
# diffs to nothing legible, and it is regenerable from the CSVs and the scripts.
# See docs/REPRODUCING.md for which script writes which file.
outputs/*
!outputs/*.csv
!outputs/stress_model_build.npz
!outputs/stress_model_three_sensor.npz
!outputs/stress_model_wide.npz
!outputs/nis_manifest.json
!outputs/gates_manifest.json
!outputs/native_order_robustness.json
!outputs/regulation_matched_tasks.json
!outputs/partial_order_v2/
outputs/partial_order_v2/*
!outputs/partial_order_v2/partial_order_synthetic_demo.json
!outputs/partial_order_v2/partial_order_synthetic_summary.csv
!outputs/state_allocation/
outputs/state_allocation/*
!outputs/state_allocation/synthetic_demo/
outputs/state_allocation/synthetic_demo/*
!outputs/state_allocation/synthetic_demo/state_allocation.csv
!outputs/state_allocation/synthetic_demo/state_allocation.json
!outputs/state_allocation/synthetic_demo/state_distribution.json

# The producer digests. Tracked, and that is the entire point: `check_output_freshness`
# compares modification times, and `git checkout` stamps every file with the checkout time,
# so on any clone the mtime gate passes vacuously on content that may not match the code.
# On 2026-08-31 it reported 284/284 while eight committed artefacts disagreed with their own
# scripts. A digest committed beside the tables survives the clone that defeats an mtime.
# Rewrite it with `python3 scripts/audit_reproducibility.py --restamp` after regenerating.
!outputs/provenance.json

# The story set from scripts/make_figures.py: the four figures the project's argument
# rests on. These are tracked, unlike the report set in figures/, because they are the
# figures a reader is pointed at from docs/FIGURES.md and because each one is a claim --
# a figure whose intervals move should show up in a review the same way a table's numbers
# do. Both formats: the SVG is the source of record and the PNG is what a slide or a
# poster can take. The pattern is deliberately narrow rather than `!outputs/*.png`, so
# that other images landing in this directory -- one-off analysis plots, the ATP-sensor
# renders -- stay out of the repository, as everything generated here does by default.
# Regenerate: python3 scripts/make_figures.py
!outputs/fig[0-9][0-9]_*.svg
!outputs/fig[0-9][0-9]_*.png

# data/plates/ is deliberately NOT listed here. It is the only wet-lab measurement in the
# repository that is tracked, and the reason it can be is that it holds the numbers and
# nothing else: 243 kB of text against 989 kB of .xlsx, no author, no revision history, no
# workbook metadata. The workbooks themselves stay out permanently -- each one names a
# private individual in docProps/core.xml, and committing that publishes it into git
# history where removing it costs a rewrite. Rebuild and re-check the text with
#   python scripts/plate_readings.py --export     # needs YSTWIN_PLATES
# which refuses to leave an export in place unless every block round-trips bit for bit
# against the workbook it came from. docs/REPRODUCING.md section 7 has the schema.

# ModelSEED and pytfa tables for bridge/thermodynamic.py. 19 MB, third-party, unchanged
# since download. Re-fetch: aliases.tsv is Unique_ModelSEED_Compound_Aliases.tsv and
# compounds.tsv is compounds.tsv, both from Biochemistry/ in the ModelSEED/
# ModelSEEDDatabase repository; thermo_data.thermodb ships inside pytfa (pip install
# pytfa==0.9.1, or data/ in the EPFL-LCSB/pytfa repository). Set YSTWIN_THERMO to point
# elsewhere. Tests skip when absent.
# TRACKED since 2026-09-11. The 18 MB of ModelSEED/pytfa tables used to be ignored on the
# grounds that they are third-party and re-fetchable, but "tests skip when absent" meant a
# fresh clone silently ran fewer checks than the author's machine. A repository whose
# thermodynamic tests only execute where someone has already downloaded the data is not
# reproducible. YSTWIN_THERMO still redirects to a local copy.

# BioNumbers Nov 2024 export (Milo lab, via Kaggle), used by
# tests/test_constants_against_bionumbers.py to check the generator's constants against a
# source that never saw this model. Re-fetch from the Kaggle BioNumbers dataset, or query
# bionumbers.hms.harvard.edu for the BNIDs that test names. conditions_annotation.csv came
# with it and nothing reads it. Tests skip when absent.
# TRACKED since 2026-09-11, same reason as data/thermo: the BioNumbers check is worth more
# running everywhere than saving 1.1 MB.

# The 2026-07-24 qPCR replicate, rebuilt from an export whose zip entries the instrument
# software lowercased. Re-create with:
#   python scripts/repair_qpcr_export.py <original Cq export> \
#       data/qpcr_repaired/20260724_ER_Ox_rep1_Cq.xlsx
# The original is a wet-lab file, not ours to publish. Set YSTWIN_QPCR to point elsewhere.
data/qpcr_repaired/

# ATP-sensor Synergy exports for the 2026-08-05 and 2026-08-09 plates, 1.7 MB, renamed by
# hand from the reader's own filenames (20260805_ATP-sensor.xlsx and the 08-09 equivalent).
# Wet-lab data: obtain from the team rather than re-fetching. Set YSTWIN_ATP_SENSOR to
# point elsewhere. docs/ATP_SENSOR.md describes what they contain; tests skip when absent.
data/atp_sensor/

# The genome-scale models. The gzipped copies ARE tracked: 21 MB of SBML between them is
# 670 kB compressed, cobrapy reads .xml.gz directly, and the earlier note here reasoned
# from the uncompressed size and so made every FBA result unreproducible by anyone without
# the sibling checkout. Both are published models -- yeast-GEM v9.0.2 from
# SysBioChalmers/yeast-GEM, ecYeastGEM_batch from SysBioChalmers/ecModels (built on
# yeast-GEM 8.3.4; the mismatch is real, see paths.ec_yeast_gem). Uncompressed drops are
# still ignored so a local .xml does not get committed by accident.
data/gem/*
!data/gem/*.xml.gz
!data/gem/MANIFEST.md
!data/gem/stress_pathway_coverage.tsv
!data/gem/absent_stress_genes.tsv
!data/gem/stress_response_map.json
# TRACKED since 2026-09-11. All eight of its paths were already declared in
# implementation_workflow.json inventory_coverage.W03; only this line kept them out.

# scraped papers and API scratch
juxie2004.txt
s1.json
.DS_Store

# Rendered figures and the HTML report: build artefacts, regenerated by
# scripts/make_figures.py and scripts/build_report.py from the tables in outputs/.
# The SVGs are the source of record and stay tracked; the PNGs and the 756 kB HTML
# do not, since they are derived from the SVGs and inflate every diff.
figures/*.png
figures/report.html

# Public data for docs/EXTERNAL_VALIDATION.md: 23 MB across GEO and SGD,
# both third-party and both unchanged since download, so neither is tracked. The tables
# the write-up quotes are in outputs/external_*.csv, which are. Re-fetch with:
#   python scripts/fetch_external_validation_data.py
# That pulls GSE18's nine series matrices and eight platform annotations from GEO, and one
# JSON of curated regulation records per transcription factor from SGD.
# TRACKED since 2026-09-11. 22 MB of GEO and SGD. Previously ignored because the quoted
# tables live in outputs/external_*.csv anyway -- but that left the inputs behind those
# tables unavailable, so nobody could re-derive them without re-running the fetch.

# Machine-local data paths; see .ystwin.env.example. Never tracked -- it names
# directories outside the checkout and, on the lab machine, a private workbook.
.ystwin.env

# Scraped pages from literature hunts. These land in the working directory when a paper is
# chased by hand and they are never inputs to anything: four of them -- nsp.html,
# nsppub.html, ajvdv.html and ouci.html -- reached the repository inside two unrelated
# commits and sat there for a week. One was a 122 kB publication list, one was a redirect
# stub, and one was the sixteen bytes "error code: 502". Nothing imported, read or cited
# any of them. Removed 2026-08-31; ignored here so the next hunt cannot repeat it.
#
# A citation belongs in data/citations/ as a resolved DOI or PMID, which is what
# scripts/refresh_citations.py maintains and tests/test_citations.py checks.
# Root only, so docs/, data/ and outputs/ are untouched -- nothing tracked lives at the
# top level in any of these three formats, and the negation that was here for README.md
# was noise, since .md matches none of these patterns.
/*.html
/*.json
/*.txt
!/requirements-quality.txt

# Ruff cache. Never belonged in the tree; removed 2026-09-03.
.ruff_cache/

# Claude Code worktrees and agent scratch
.claude/

.env
.env.local
.env.*.local
data/native_law_v2/granados/sealed/
data/native_law_v2/granados/development/private/
data/holdout_transfer/source_index.json
